      "dom_id": "question-23981",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following shows the graph of $y={x}^{2}-3x+5?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y={x}^{2}-3x+5.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=-1,0,1,2,3,4$ and then use the formula $y={x}^{2}-3x+5$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $9$ | $5$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245131/assets/5a7374c0e81f36d4d320abf38dcf72ac0b377c335894c601bfae82b64ea0bca3.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245131/assets/f671cee2e37f7905c220357782d2d4f2e6361d4858030084492e216d3f3031cd.png)",
