    {
      "dom_id": "question-83559",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245131/assets/ecd951a63d500d0bf6b5234a65b8df55ada27882be337df6aea7d7aaa516e91b.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}x+k \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we can find that $k=\\frac{1}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at \n$(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$",
